meta {
  name: GET /api/resource with Bearer token
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{apiBaseUrl}}/api/resource
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
  Content-Type: application/json
  Origin: {{redirectUri}}
}

docs {
  # 3 - The protected call: bearer token -> Spring Boot resource server

  Arrow 5 in Figure 2. This is exactly what `api.js -> callApi()` sends from the
  browser: the `access_token` from request 2, as a `Bearer` credential, at
  `GET /api/resource`.

  ## Verified 2026-09-30 with a real, live access_token

  A fresh CLI run of this collection starts with `accessToken` empty (auth
  requires interactive password+MFA, which this preset cannot script), so a
  standalone `bru run` of just this file gets a correct 401, not the 200 shown
  below - see the pre-request warning.

  What WAS proven end to end, against the running containers
  (`sso-spa`/`sso-resource-server`) and the real tenant: signed in once at
  `http://localhost:3100`, pulled the MSAL-issued `access_token` straight out
  of the SPA's own `sessionStorage` (undisputably the real token response,
  since it's what MSAL itself cached after doing the exchange), and sent it
  here.

  - `GET /api/resource` -> **200**, `audience: ["api://6841df0b-...bb5"]`,
    `roles: ["Resource.Admin"]`, real `subject`/`name` claims back.
  - `GET /api/admin` with the same token -> **200** (the token carried
    `Resource.Admin`).
  - `GET /api/resource` with no `Authorization` header -> **401** (negative
    control, same run).
  - `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: http://localhost:3100` present on every
    response - CORS is real, not just configured.

  So: run this preset standalone and see 401 - that's this preset behaving
  correctly with no captured token, not a failure of the stack. To reproduce
  the 200, paste a live `access_token` into the `accessToken` environment
  variable (pulled from the SPA's sessionStorage as above), or run requests 1
  and 2 with a real interactive sign-in first.

  `Origin` is set deliberately so this exercises the **same CORS path the browser
  uses** - the resource server allowlists `http://localhost:3100` only.

  ## Note on the path

  It is `/api/resource`, **singular**. `ResourceController` maps `@RequestMapping("/api")`
  + `@GetMapping("/resource")`. There is no `/api/resources` - that path returns
  401, because Spring Security's `anyRequest().authenticated()` rejects unmatched
  routes before routing, which makes a typo look identical to an auth failure.

  ## What the 200 proves

  Reaching the controller at all means the token passed **four independent
  checks** in `SecurityConfig.jwtDecoder()` - the response body shows the
  evidence for each:

  | Check | Enforced by | Visible in the body as |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | RS256 signature vs Entra ID's JWKS | `NimbusJwtDecoder` (keys cached, arrow 6) | reaching the controller at all |
  | Not expired / not before | `JwtValidators.createDefault()` | `expiresAt` |
  | Issuer is this tenant | `TenantIssuerValidator` | - |
  | Audience is this API | `AudienceValidator` | `audience` |

  Plus `scopes` (from `scp`) and `roles` - the latter is what gates
  `GET /api/admin` at 403 vs 200.

  ## Evidence to capture

  Run it and keep: the **200**, the `audience` field matching the API client ID,
  and `subject` / `preferredUsername` identifying the signed-in user. That
  triplet is the proof the full chain worked end to end - a token minted by
  Entra ID for *this* user and *this* API was independently validated by the
  resource server with no call back to Microsoft on the request path (NFR-2).

  ## If you get a 401

  | Cause | Tell |
  | --- | --- |
  | `accessToken` empty | Request 2 was not run, or failed. |
  | Token expired (~1h) | Re-run requests 1-2. |
  | You sent the **id_token** | `aud` is the SPA client ID, not the API. The classic mistake. |
  | Wrong `aud` | The scope in request 1/2 was not `{{apiScope}}`. |

  Check `docker compose logs resource-server` - the chain logs the specific
  rejection reason at DEBUG.
}

script:pre-request {
  if (!bru.getVar('accessToken')) {
    console.log('WARNING: accessToken is empty - run request 2 first. Expect a 401.');
  }
}

script:post-response {
  const b = res.getBody();
  console.log('HTTP', res.getStatus());
  if (res.getStatus() === 200 && b) {
    console.log('subject          :', b.subject);
    console.log('preferredUsername:', b.preferredUsername);
    console.log('audience         :', JSON.stringify(b.audience), '<- validated by AudienceValidator');
    console.log('scopes           :', JSON.stringify(b.scopes));
    console.log('roles            :', JSON.stringify(b.roles));
    console.log('expiresAt        :', b.expiresAt);
  } else {
    console.log('WWW-Authenticate:', res.getHeader('www-authenticate') || '(none)');
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

tests {
  test("bearer token is accepted - 200", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("response is the protected payload", function() {
    expect(res.getBody().message).to.be.a('string').and.not.empty;
  });

  test("audience on the validated token is this API", function() {
    const apiId = bru.getEnvVar('apiClientId');
    const aud = res.getBody().audience || [];
    const ok = aud.includes(apiId) || aud.includes(`api://${apiId}`);
    expect(ok, `audience was ${JSON.stringify(aud)}`).to.be.true;
  });

  test("the call identifies the signed-in user", function() {
    expect(res.getBody().subject).to.be.a('string').and.not.empty;
  });

  test("CORS allows the SPA origin", function() {
    expect(res.getHeader('access-control-allow-origin')).to.equal(bru.getEnvVar('redirectUri'));
  });
}
